<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Skorta</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Skorta was the medieval name of the mountainous district of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karytaina">Karytaina</a> and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alpheios">Alpheios</a> valley. Its Greek population was hard to control, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> held it through the barony of Karytaina. Byzantine forces recovered much of it in 1320.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name Skorta, in French Escorta, was used in the 13th and 14th centuries, during Frankish rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>. It denoted the mountainous western half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a>, which separated the coastal plains of the western and south-western Peloponnese from the Arcadian plateau in the interior. The name is found chiefly in the editions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea">Chronicle of the Morea</a> in several languages, and the chronicle of Pseudo-Dorotheos of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monemvasia">Monemvasia</a> gives it as Skodra and Skorda.</p><p>The northern part of the district, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akova">Akova</a>, was also known by the Greek name Mesarea, a common term in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> for inland places. Where the two names stand side by side, Skorta is limited to the southern part, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karytaina">Karytaina</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The local population was known for its rebellious character and never fully submitted to the Frankish Princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>. It rose up often, aided by the Byzantine Greeks of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mystras</a>. To control the region the princes set up two of the most powerful baronies of Achaea, the Barony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akova">Akova</a> in the north and the Barony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karytaina">Karytaina</a>, also called the barony of Skorta, in the south.</p><p>Around 1302 Skorta rebelled when Prince Philip of Savoy raised new taxes on the local Greek gentry. The rebels took advantage of the absence of the principality's marshal, Nicholas III of Saint Omer, who was campaigning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> with many troops. With help from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mystras</a> they took and burned the castles of Saint Helen and Crèvecoeur and laid siege to the castle of Beaufort. When the prince marched against them with the levies of his feudatories, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> withdrew, and Frankish rule over the region was quickly restored.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Skorta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the fortress of Saint George in <b>Skorta</b>, the forces of the Byzantine governor of Mystras, Andronikos Asen, ambushed and defeated those of the Latin Principality of Achaea.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-9">9 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The barony of Karytaina, also called <b>Skorta</b> after its mountainous district, was created around 1209 in the Principality of Achaea and granted to Hugh of Bruyeres.</blockquote><a href="/state/barony-of-karytaina">Barony of Karytaina</a></li></ul></section>
